-- title:
--   Crossing counts are determined by the profile
-- statement:
--   Fix $s\in\mathbb{N}$. Colors are $0,\dots,s$ and a coloring of a finite set $X$ is a map $a:X\to\{0,\dots,s\}$. For $i<s$, $P_i(z)=\{x:z(x)\le i\}$, $d_i=2^{-(i+1)}$, the crossings are $|P_i(z)\setminus P_i(a)|$, the moved row $i$ is $\{x:z(x)=i<a(x)\}$, and the movement is $\sum_x(a(x)-z(x))$. The profile $n_{a,z}(i,h)$ counts points with final color $i$ and original color $h>i$. For colorings $a,z$ and $i<s$,
--   $$
--   |P_i(z)\setminus P_i(a)|=\sum_{j\le i<h}n_{a,z}(j,h) .
--   $$
--
--   Admissibility tests therefore depend on the original coloring only through the profile. This is what allows one common witness for a whole profile group.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ExpectationThreshold/SelectorProfile.lean#L108-L174

